  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/e328914d55b82fdd07147f683aa44aebbc3f3e908f180e6632332119c81ce482.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the line $y=-1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on this line. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{1}{y}$ is never zero.\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{\u2032}={e}^{y+1}$ is never zero.\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{\u2032}=x(y+1)$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\"",
